/candidate/codex-trace/2026/07/23/rollout-2026-07-23T15-06-40-019f9096-5a4a-7812-b2ca-596462a1d46f.jsonl:315:{"timestamp":"2026-07-23T20:25:04.393Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_004b779d384db21b016a6278665c4481a196fa428835558a31","status":"completed","call_id":"call_QuaoOigOUP8HRxL1XS9SS1YJ","name":"apply_patch","input":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /work/PROOF.md\n+VALIDATED\n+\n+# What is proven\n+\n+Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, `intersection` is partially correct for\n+every pair of two-integer tuples `(A0, A1)` and `(B0, B1)` satisfying\n+`A0 <= A1` and `B0 <= B1`.\n+\n+Let\n+\n+```text\n+L = min(A1, B1) - max(A0, B0).\n+```\n+\n+If the call terminates, it returns `\"YES\"` exactly when `L >= 2` and no integer\n+`d` with `2 <= d < L` divides `L`; otherwise it returns `\"NO\"`.  The entry\n+claim also requires the call to restore its caller environment, scope\n+allocator, heap, heap allocator, stack, return state, exception state, and exit\n+code to the values shown in `spec.k`.\n+\n+This is a partial-correctness theorem, as prescribed by the Kit workflow.  It\n+does not itself assert termination.\n+\n+# Formal claims\n+\n+`SPEC.divisor-loop` is the loop circularity.  At the actual recurring\n+`#while` control term, with `2 <= I <= N`, it proves that the divisor counter\n+ends at `N` and the Boolean flag ends as:\n+\n+```text\n+P andBool noDivisors(N, I)\n+```\n+\n+The claim pins the exact seven-key plain function frame used by this program.\n+Its framed continuation is preserved.\n+\n+`SPEC.intersection` binds `\"intersection\"` to the exact translated closure\n+body, supplies two symbolic two-integer tuples, assumes the two interval-order\n+conditions, executes the call through the fixed call semantics, and reaches:\n+\n+```text\n+primeResult(minInt(A1, B1) -Int maxInt(A0, B0))\n+```\n+\n+The complete two-claim proof is required: filtering out the loop claim removes\n+the circularity needed by the entry proof.\n+\n+# Proof-extension inventory\n+\n+## `intersectionBody`\n+\n+- Class: definitional summary.\n+- Semantic role: a zero-argument abbreviation for the exact `Stmts` tree in\n+  `solution.mpy`; it supplies the closure binding but does not replace any\n+  program execution.\n+- Domain and context: the one unconditional equation, used as the body of the\n+  closure bound at module scope in the entry claim.\n+- Justification scope and containment: exactly the translated target body.\n+  `prove.sh` regenerates `solution.mpy`; the body in `verification.k` was\n+  audited constructor-for-constructor against it.\n+- State footprint: none.  The resulting body executes with the fixed `MPY`\n+  lookup, argument evaluation, call, assignment, loop, return, and frame-pop\n+  rules.\n+- Value/control influence: it determines all target control and the returned\n+  value through real execution.\n+- Validation: `spec-body-mutation.k` replaces this body with\n+  `return \"NO\"`; the prime witness then reaches `\"NO\"` and the requested\n+  `\"YES\"` claim is rejected with exit 1.\n+- Dependents: `SPEC.intersection`.\n+\n+## `noDivisors(Int, Int)`\n+\n+- Class: definitional summary.\n+- Semantic role: names the mathematical fact that no divisor occurs in the\n+  half-open range `[I, N)`; it does not rewrite a Python program term.\n+- Complete domain: all mathematical integer pairs.\n+- Equations: `I < 2` redirects once to `I = 2`; `I >= 2 and I >= N` returns\n+  true; `I >= 2 and I < N` checks `pyMod(N, I)` and recurs at `I + 1`.\n+- Coverage/overlap/descent: the three guards are pairwise disjoint and\n+  exhaustive.  The totalizing first equation avoids modulo at zero.  The\n+  recursive case strictly increases `I` toward the base region.\n+- Matched context and state footprint: functional terms only; no cells,\n+  continuation, binding, state, exception, or control are matched or changed.\n+- Value influence: the summary fixes the final Boolean flag and therefore both\n+  return branches.\n+- Value justification: exhaustive defining equations plus the independently\n+  closing `SPEC.divisor-loop` fixed-execution claim connect one Python\n+  iteration to the recursive equation.\n+- Validation: the length-2 and length-4 opposite-result claims in\n+  `spec-vacuity.k` are both rejected and expose the actual opposite values.\n+- Dependents: `SPEC.divisor-loop`, `primeResult`, and `SPEC.intersection`.\n+\n+## `primeResult(Int)`\n+\n+- Class: definitional summary.\n+- Semantic role: maps the standard primality predicate to the two required\n+  string values; it does not replace execution.\n+- Complete domain: all mathematical integers.\n+- Equations: `\"YES\"` under `N >= 2 and noDivisors(N, 2)`; `\"NO\"` under\n+  `N < 2 or notBool noDivisors(N, 2)`.\n+- Coverage/overlap: the guards are exhaustive and disjoint.\n+- Matched context and state footprint: functional terms only; no operational\n+  cells or control.\n+- Value influence: exactly the target claim's returned value.\n+- Value justification: the equations fix both values, while fixed program\n+  execution is connected by the loop and entry claims.\n+- Validation: distinct prime and composite witnesses reject the opposite\n+  interpretations.\n+- Dependents: `SPEC.intersection`.\n+\n+## `SPEC.divisor-loop`\n+\n+- Class: derived lemma (a reachability circularity).\n+- Semantic role: proves the real Python loop under fixed semantics; it is not\n+  an operational rewrite in `verification.k`.\n+- Domain: `2 <= I <= N`, Boolean `P`, integer `N`, the exact seven-key plain\n+  local map, arbitrary enclosing scope map and parent, and the actual\n+  `#while` term.\n+- Matched context: `#while(C, B) ~> KREST` with the same arbitrary `KREST` on\n+  the destination.  All configuration cells omitted from the written claim\n+  are automatically framed unchanged by K.\n+- Context containment: the entry claim reaches precisely this plain frame and\n+  loop term.  No `\"$cells\"` binding is admitted, so closure